1
00:02:20,526 --> 00:02:26,526
The Horror, the pain and the suffering of Hiroshima!

2
00:04:41,052 --> 00:04:47,052
Hiroshima is a documentary, drama, war film directed by Paul Wilmshurst.

3
00:07:01,578 --> 00:07:07,578
The documentary recounts the world's first nuclear attack
and examines the alarming repercussions.

4
00:09:22,105 --> 00:09:28,105
Covering a three-week period from the Trinity test to the atomic bombing of Hiroshima, the program
chronicles America's political gamble and the planning for the momentous event.

5
00:11:42,631 --> 00:11:48,631
Archival film, dramatizations, and special effects feature what occurred aboard the Enola
Gay (the aircraft that dropped the bomb) and inside the exploding bomb.

6
00:14:03,157 --> 00:14:09,157
Narrator (voice), played by John Hurt.

7
00:16:23,684 --> 00:16:29,684
Self - Hiroshima Survivor, played by Shuntaro Hida.

8
00:18:44,210 --> 00:18:50,210
Winston Churchill, played by Robert Austin.

9
00:21:04,736 --> 00:21:10,736
Parsons, played by George Anton.

10
00:23:25,263 --> 00:23:31,263
Self - Military Advisor, played by George Elsey.

11
00:25:45,789 --> 00:25:51,789
Self - Pilot, Enola Gay, played by Paul Tibbets.

12
00:28:06,315 --> 00:28:12,315
On 6 and 9 August 1945, the United States detonated two atomic bombs over the Japanese
cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, respectively, during World War II.

13
00:30:26,842 --> 00:30:32,842
The aerial bombings killed 150,000 to 246,000 people, most of whom were civilians,
and remain the only uses of nuclear weapons in an armed conflict.

14
00:32:47,368 --> 00:32:53,368
Japan announced its surrender to the Allies on 15 August, six days after the bombing of Nagasaki
and the Soviet Union's declaration of war against Japan and invasion of Manchuria.

15
00:35:07,894 --> 00:35:13,894
The Japanese government signed an instrument of
surrender on 2 September, ending the war.

16
00:37:28,421 --> 00:37:34,421
In the final year of World War II, the Allies prepared for a
costly invasion of the Japanese mainland.

17
00:39:48,947 --> 00:39:54,947
This undertaking was preceded by a conventional bombing and firebombing campaign
that devastated 64 Japanese cities, including an operation on Tokyo.

18
00:42:09,473 --> 00:42:15,473
The war in Europe concluded when Germany surrendered on 8 May 1945, and
the Allies turned their full attention to the Pacific War.

19
00:44:29,999 --> 00:44:35,999
By July 1945, the Allies' Manhattan Project had produced two types of atomic bombs: "Little Boy", an enriched
uranium gun-type fission weapon, and "Fat Man", a plutonium implosion-type nuclear weapon.

20
00:46:50,526 --> 00:46:56,526
The 509th Composite Group of the U.S.

21
00:49:11,052 --> 00:49:17,052
Army Air Forces was trained and equipped with the specialized Silverplate version of the
Boeing B-29 Superfortress, and deployed to Tinian in the Mariana Islands.

22
00:51:31,578 --> 00:51:37,578
The Allies called for the unconditional surrender of the Imperial Japanese Armed Forces in the
Potsdam Declaration on 26 July 1945, the alternative being "prompt and utter destruction".

23
00:53:52,105 --> 00:53:58,105
The Japanese government ignored the ultimatum.

24
00:56:12,631 --> 00:56:18,631
The consent of the United Kingdom was obtained for the bombing, as was required by the
Quebec Agreement, and orders were issued on 25 July by General Thomas T.

25
00:58:33,157 --> 00:58:39,157
Handy, the acting chief of staff of the U.S.

26
01:00:53,684 --> 01:00:59,684
Army, for atomic bombs to be used on Hiroshima, Kokura, Niigata, and Nagasaki.

27
01:03:14,210 --> 01:03:20,210
These targets were chosen because they were large urban areas
that also held significant military facilities.

28
01:05:34,736 --> 01:05:40,736
On 6 August, a Little Boy was dropped on Hiroshima.

29
01:07:55,263 --> 01:08:01,263
Three days later, a Fat Man was dropped on Nagasaki.

30
01:10:15,789 --> 01:10:21,789
Over the next two to four months, the effects of the atomic bombings killed 90,000 to 166,000 people in
Hiroshima and 60,000 to 80,000 people in Nagasaki; roughly half the deaths occurred on the first day.

31
01:12:36,315 --> 01:12:42,315
For months afterward, many people continued to die from the effects of burns, radiation
sickness, and other injuries, compounded by illness and malnutrition.

32
01:14:56,842 --> 01:15:02,842
Despite Hiroshima's sizable military garrison, estimated at
24,000 troops, some 90% of the dead were civilians.

33
01:17:17,368 --> 01:17:23,368
Scholars have extensively studied the effects of the bombings on the social and political character of subsequent world history and popular culture, and there is still much
debate concerning the ethical and legal justification for the bombings as well as their ramifications of geopolitics especially with the context of the Cold War.

34
01:19:37,894 --> 01:19:43,894
Supporters argue that the atomic bombings were necessary to bring an end to the war with minimal casualties and ultimately prevented a greater loss of life
on both sides, and also assert that the demonstration of atomic weaponry created the Long Peace in the fear of preventing a nuclear war.

35
01:21:58,421 --> 01:22:04,421
Conversely, critics argue that the bombings were unnecessary for the war's end and were a war crime, raising moral and ethical implications,
and also assert that future use of atomic weaponry is more likely than anticipated and could lead to a nuclear holocaust.

36
01:24:18,947 --> 01:24:24,947
The film explores themes of atomic bomb, radiation,
world war ii, interview, hiroshima, japan.

37
01:26:39,473 --> 01:26:45,473
The tone is emotional.
